  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Angola El Ni&#xF1;o Response &#x2013; Community-based Management of Acute Malnutrition and Assistance to Vulnerable and Food Insecure Families in Hu&#xED;la and Cunene provinces</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The main objective of this WFP project  provides life-saving food assistance to families severely affected by drought and implements a community management of acute malnutrition (CMAM) service through house-to-house visits. This includes treating moderate acute malnutrition (MAM) in children under five to prevent severe acute malnutrition (SAM) outbreaks, in coordination with UNICEF. Community health workers (CHWs) receive refresher training in nutritional screening, MAM treatment, and basic WASH counselling, as well as SAM case referral. Vulnerable families receive commodity vouchers for essential food supplies, and children receive Ready-to-Use Supplementary Food, with the intervention spanning three cycles of two months each. The project provides humanitarian assistance to  98,000 people, including 6,563 men, 7,000 women and 84,437  children including 2,450  Persons with disabilities .</narrative>
  </description>
